issues.problematic_code_snippet:
if (ret == AOF_OPEN_ERR || ret == AOF_FAILED || ret == AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED) {
    goto cleanup;
}

findings.body:
The `fmterr` recovery truncates to `valid_up_to`, but `valid_up_to` is advanced after *every* command, including commands queued inside an open MULTI. If the crash left `MULTI` plus some queued commands and then zero-filled bytes (typical after power loss), the parser hits `fmterr` while the fake client still has `CLIENT_MULTI` set. The file is truncated inside the transaction, with no `EXEC`.

On the next startup, every write appended after recovery lands inside that dangling MULTI. At EOF the existing code sees `CLIENT_MULTI` and sets `valid_up_to = valid_before_multi` (line 1673), then `uxeof` truncates the whole block when `aof-load-truncated` is yes (the default). So all writes made after the recovery are discarded with only a 'Revert incomplete MULTI/EXEC' warning. The MULTI-revert logic only runs on the clean-EOF path, not on the `fmterr` path this PR adds.

::code{file="src/aof.c" lines="1658-1674"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The recovery branch must not leave the file inside an open transaction. Fix it in the recovery branch, not just on clean EOF.

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1724-1746"}

`valid_up_to` is initialized to 0 (line 1488) and only updated after an AOF command runs. When the base file starts with an RDB preamble, `rdbLoadRio` succeeds, but `valid_up_to` is not updated to the preamble end. If the tail after the preamble is garbage, `fmterr` runs with `valid_up_to == 0`.

The size check becomes `st_size - 0 < max-size`. For any base file smaller than 4096 bytes (a small dataset with `aof-use-rdb-preamble yes`), the code calls `truncate(path, 0)`, deleting the entire RDB preamble and therefore the whole dataset. It logs 'AOF loaded anyway' and starts with an empty database. No existing test covers an RDB-format base with a corrupt tail.

::code{file="src/aof.c" lines="1541-1552"}

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1727-1731"}

The `fmterr` branch truncates the file on disk immediately. The 'not last file' rule is applied later in `loadAppendOnlyFiles`, where the result becomes `AOF_FAILED`. The server then exits, but the middle file has already lost the bytes after its last good command.

If an operator restarts after that failure, the middle file is now valid and the server may start. The data removed from that file is gone without any further warning, even though the first startup refused to load. The tests show this: the 'Intermediate AOF is broken' test truncates `appendonly.aof.1.incr.aof` before exiting. The same applies to any chain where the recovered file is not last.

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1727-1731"}

::code{file="src/aof.c" lines="1836-1839"}

The swap block at lines 827-829 renames the files so the previously corrupted `mid` file becomes the last file. But the corruption was in `mid` (the 'CORRUPTION' line), and the earlier startup in the same test already truncated `mid` on disk through `fmterr` recovery. The final start therefore loads two clean files. It passes even if recovery of a corrupted last file is broken.

The test name says it covers recovery of a corrupted last file, so it should recreate the corruption on the file that ends up last.

::code{file="tests/integration/aof.tcl" lines="827-838"}

`loadAppendOnlyFiles()` can now return `AOF_BROKEN_RECOVERED`. `DEBUG LOADAOF` in `src/debug.c` accepts only `AOF_OK` and `AOF_EMPTY`, so a recovered load replies with 'Error trying to load the AOF files' even though the database was loaded. The client sees a failure and the data is actually in memory.

The same return value also reaches the cleanup path, where `stopLoading(ret == AOF_OK || ret == AOF_TRUNCATED)` passes 0. That fires `REDISMODULE_SUBEVENT_LOADING_FAILED` for a load that succeeded. This PR did not update either caller.

::code{file="src/debug.c" lines="598-601"}

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1830-1832"}

`valid_up_to` is initialized to 0 (line 1488) and is only set from `ftello(fp)` in the command loop. It is never set to -1 on a path that reaches `fmterr`. So the 'Last valid command offset is invalid' branch in the new `fmterr` code cannot run in practice. It copies the same branch from `uxeof` (line 1694), which is also effectively unreachable there.

It is harmless, but it reads as if -1 were a real state. If you keep it, add a comment, or remove it along with the matching `uxeof` branch.

::code{file="src/aof.c" lines="1725-1727"}
